## Contemporary Global Affairs Magazine

The visual personality should be:

- Confident.
- Intelligent.
- Contemporary.
- International.
- Editorial.
- Expressive.
- Refined.
- Trustworthy.
- Memorable.

The current warm editorial foundation can remain.

However, the result must be much richer than a beige background and a sequence of text blocks.

Develop a visual language with:

- Distinctive editorial imagery.
- Strong headline composition.
- Asymmetric layouts.
- Powerful feature-story presentation.
- Different story formats.
- More expressive use of color.
- Rich editorial surfaces.
- Thoughtful whitespace.
- Sophisticated reading typography.

## 4.1 Keep a coherent palette

Retain the existing semantic palette as a starting point:

- Warm ivory.
- Ink black.
- Deep navy.
- Signal vermilion.
- World blue.
- AI violet.
- Economy green.

Refine its contrast and relationships.

Avoid introducing arbitrary new colors.

Use domain colors for recognizable editorial identity, not as decoration everywhere.

The cover can use a darker visual environment while the rest of the page retains a warm editorial canvas.

Both themes must be beautifully art-directed.

## 4.2 Remove sterile repetition

Reduce reliance on:

- Repeated horizontal rules.
- Identical white panels.
- Small technical metadata.
- The same title/description/card pattern.
- Equal-weight three-column grids.
- Repeated circular graphic motifs.
- Generic rounded cards.

Do not remove every border.

Use borders only where they help establish structure.

Make typography, image composition, and whitespace the main tools for hierarchy.

